{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>You shall not take interest from your brother.</b> This is a warning to the borrower. (Leviticus 25:37) ״את כספך לא תתן״ — that is a warning to the lender. And interest is called נשך from the language of (Ecclesiastes 10:11) ״אם ישוך הנחש״, to teach that just as the serpent's venom enters from the biter into the bitten, into his limbs and sinews, so too the punishment of the forbidden enters the property of the lender who takes interest. <b>נשך כל דבר</b> — interest of anything: even speech is forbidden. Thus they expounded: from where do we learn that if one is owed a maneh by another, and the borrower is not accustomed to greet him first, that it is forbidden for him to greet him first? As it is said, ״נשך כל דבר״ — even speech is forbidden. And this is a general principle for all other benefits and advantages that the borrower is forbidden to confer, unless he was accustomed to them before the loan."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:38.757827616Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}